WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:01.760
Good morning Hank, it's Tuesday.

00:00:01.760 --> 00:00:04.100
What do I have in common with a Catholic priest?

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:05.800
I'd just feel naked without my collar.

00:00:05.900 --> 00:00:08.820
It's Question Tuesday, the day that I answer real question from real Nerdfighter

00:00:08.820 --> 00:00:10.160
Just the one question today:

00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:13.480
"I'm a college freshman, how do I figure out what the hell to do with my life?"

00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:15.680
Hank, we get some version of this question almost every day,

00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:19.000
but to answer the question, I have to begin by disagreeing with it.

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:23.020
The whole "what do I do with my life?" question implies that adulthood is this monolithic creature

00:00:23.060 --> 00:00:25.480
that like, you acquire your job and your spouse,

00:00:25.480 --> 00:00:29.580
and then you just ride it out until Time's winged chariot shows up.

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:31.480
I thought this when I was younger too, and it's not our fault,

00:00:31.480 --> 00:00:34.240
because how does every single happily ever after movie end?

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:38.380
With finding your one true love and kissing them, occasionally marrying them,

00:00:38.380 --> 00:00:40.880
at the very latest having a child with them.

00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:44.440
95% of romantic stories end with partners committing to each other,

00:00:44.460 --> 00:00:49.360
because everything after that is just the blank, terrifying morass of adulthood,

00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:53.040
this monotonous grind in which the only real excitement in your life

00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:57.020
is occasionally finding a truly ripe avocado at the grocery store.

00:00:57.020 --> 00:01:01.300
Now listen, Hank, I'm not going to lie to you, I get very excited when I find a truly ripe avocado at the grocery store

00:01:01.300 --> 00:01:03.260
but it's not the only excitement in my life!

00:01:03.260 --> 00:01:07.440
And let me submit that one of the reasons you're so terrified of figuring out what you're going to do with your life

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:10.440
is that you think you're only going to be able to do one thing!

00:01:10.520 --> 00:01:15.580
Like if someone told you that in four years you were going to have to pick one musical note to listen to for the rest of your life,

00:01:15.580 --> 00:01:19.420
you would spend a lot of time being super anxious about which note to pick

00:01:19.420 --> 00:01:21.960
and the various tonal qualities of different notes.

00:01:22.040 --> 00:01:23.980
But that's not what life is like!

00:01:23.980 --> 00:01:28.400
You will always be figuring out what to do with your life, and you'll get to do lots of different things,

00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:31.000
and your decisions will be unmade by circumstance,

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:38.000
like your wife gets a job in Kuala Lumpur, or you're staring at the night sky one night and decide you wanna get a PhD in astrophysics

00:01:38.060 --> 00:01:40.640
Or your uncle dies and there's no one to take over his junkyard

00:01:40.680 --> 00:01:43.720
or your little brother starts a frickin' video conference...

00:01:43.820 --> 00:01:48.120
Just like your life now, adulthood will have all this tension between idealism and practicality,

00:01:48.160 --> 00:01:50.580
and you'll have to balance competing desires.

00:01:50.580 --> 00:01:51.700
I mean, give yourself some credit!

00:01:51.700 --> 00:01:55.480
Even if you're 14 or 15, you're already kind of doing adulthood.

00:01:55.480 --> 00:01:58.860
But I think what actually scared me about choosing whether to go to school and what to study

00:01:58.860 --> 00:02:01.920
was that each time I made a decision, my options narrowed.

00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:04.980
Right, like if you're pre-med, you're probably not gonna be a software designer,

00:02:04.980 --> 00:02:08.800
and if you study archaeology you're probably not going to become an electrical engineer,

00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:11.480
and there is something a little terrifying about the narrowing of those possibilities

00:02:11.480 --> 00:02:14.640
because you become aware that they will continue to narrow until you are dead.

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:18.780
And that sucks, but here's the other thing, if you don't make decisions, the options still narrow!

00:02:18.860 --> 00:02:24.140
And you don't have to do just one thing, you can be an electrical engineer who volunteers on archaeological digs!

00:02:24.140 --> 00:02:26.960
William Carlos Williams was both a poet and a doctor,

00:02:26.960 --> 00:02:32.440
Julia Child, aside from being an extraordinary chef, was also a freaking spy for the United States,

00:02:32.440 --> 00:02:35.460
I mean, Hank, if you told my college self that I'd one day be a novelist

00:02:35.460 --> 00:02:39.380
with a side gig putting my mustachioed face on the torsos of strangers,

00:02:39.380 --> 00:02:41.420
I would have been duly surprised.

00:02:41.420 --> 00:02:45.640
And you studied organic fracking chemistry before writing songs about Harry Potter.

00:02:45.640 --> 00:02:48.660
So here's my advice: study broadly and without fear.

00:02:48.820 --> 00:02:51.460
Learn a language if you can, because that will make your life more interesting.

00:02:51.520 --> 00:02:52.780
Read a little bit every day.

00:02:52.780 --> 00:02:57.480
But most importantly, try to surround yourself with people you like and make cool stuff with them.

00:02:57.480 --> 00:03:02.920
In the end, at least in my experience, what you do isn't going to be nearly as interesting or important as who you do it with.

00:03:03.020 --> 00:03:07.500
And Hank, that's why I always say that the best decision we made as videobloggers was also the first decision:

00:03:07.540 --> 00:03:09.060
to collaborate with each other.

00:03:09.960 --> 00:03:12.360
Oh, that sounds perilously close to me saying that I love my brother,

00:03:12.360 --> 00:03:14.400
so I need to cut the sentiment a little bit.

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:17.800
I need to think of something like maybe I'll just be like, "You have a stupid face", or something.

00:03:18.520 --> 00:03:19.540
Yeah, that's good.

00:03:19.540 --> 00:03:22.480
Hank, you have a stupid face.  I'll see you on Friday.

